{
  "dom_id": "step-q182464",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${5}^{n}+{9}^{n}+2$ is divisible by $4$ for all $n\\in N.$\n\nLet $P(n)$ denote the following sequence of statements:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,4\\,∣\\,{5}^{n}+{9}^{n}+2\n$$\n\n We proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nSubstituting $n=\\,${{field-2}} into the expression ${5}^{n}+{9}^{n}+2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-3}}={{field-4}},\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=\\,${{field-7}}.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
